===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[cat]]-sized [[marsupial]], ''[[Setonix brachyurus]]'', of southwestern [[Australia]].
#* '''2003''', John Long, ''Introduced Mammals of the World: Their History, Distribution and Influence'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=7YC3cYhGMOcC&pg=PA29&dq=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=5-AUUtWkF8WhiAe8rYGACQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&f=false page 29],
#*: At least 673 '''quokkas''' were re-introduced from Rottnest Island to the Marsupial Research Station of the University of Western Australia (254 ha) at Jandakot from 1972 to 1988.
#* '''2005''', Hugh Tyndale-Biscoe, ''Life of Marsupials'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=gDFeNMhJlPUC&pg=PA354&dq=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=5-AUUtWkF8WhiAe8rYGACQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&f=false page 354],
#*: Tammars, '''quokkas''' and black-footed rock wallabies inhabit islands in the southwest, while three species of hare wallaby, the burrowing bettong and five species of rock wallaby inhabit various islands in the northwest.
#* '''2012''', Ken Richardson, ''Australia's Amazing Kangaroos: Their Conservation, Unique Biology and Coexisternce with Humans'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=oVXRDFpvfkkC&pg=PA125&dq=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=5-AUUtWkF8WhiAe8rYGACQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&f=false page 125],
#*: Older unburnt areas (more than 25 years) on their own appear unable to sustain a '''quokka''' population.
#*: The largest number of '''quokkas''' occurs on Rottnest Island near Perth, where the population estimates vary from 8000 to 12,000 individuals.

